<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  org.nextbsd.wland.plist

  Boot-time launchd plist for wland, the NextBSD 802.11 management daemon.

  WHY org.nextbsd.* AND NOT com.apple.*

  The other system daemons here keep Apple's exact labels (com.apple.configd,
  com.apple.notifyd, com.apple.IPConfiguration), because a daemon that models an
  Apple service inherits Apple's label — and because Apple-derived code looks
  those labels up (a clean-room Security.framework carries
  bootstrap_look_up("com.apple.securityd") in its own source).

  Neither applies here. Apple's daemon is `wifid`; ours is `wland`. So
  com.apple.wland would be a FABRICATED Apple label for a daemon Apple never
  shipped — the worst of both worlds. And nothing Apple-derived looks this label
  up: we write the `wlan` CLI, we write the System Preferences backend, and the
  CoreWLAN-shaped ObjC API lives above the Mach line in Gershwin. Both ends are
  ours, so the label is ours.

  WHAT WLAND DOES

  1. Clones the 802.11 VAP off each radio (`ifconfig wlan0 create wlandev
     iwlwifi0` — on stock FreeBSD that is rc.conf's job, and NextBSD has no
     rc.conf and no /etc/rc.d, so it had no owner at all).
  2. Spawns and drives STOCK wpa_supplicant from base over its control socket,
     with an EMPTY config, so wland is the sole author of every network block.
  3. Publishes State:/Network/Interface/<if>/AirPort {Authenticated, SSID,
     BSSID, RSSI, Channel, Security} — which is the predicate ipconfigd's DHCP
     gate waits on. Without this key, DHCP on WLAN does not work AT ALL: net80211
     reports the link UP at association, before the WPA 4-way handshake, so
     ipconfigd would DHCP onto an unauthenticated port and every packet would be
     dropped.

  HOW WLAND FINDS RADIOS

  net80211 (Wlan.kext) publishes each radio in the IORegistry as an
  IO80211Controller entry when it attaches and retires it when it goes (a kext
  unload, a USB radio unplugged). wland follows that class with
  IOServiceAddMatchingNotification, first-match and terminated, and brings the
  radio up or releases it. With no radio it blocks and does not poll; without
  /dev/ioregistry it rescans net.wlan.devices every 2 s instead
  (nextbsd-userland#502). launchd LaunchEvents (com.apple.iokit.matching) would
  let launchd start wland only when a radio appears, but libxpc's event
  streams are not there yet, so the job still runs at load.

  RunAtLoad + KeepAlive: starts at PID-1 launchd boot and respawns on crash.
  There is NO launchd service ordering in this port (no StartAfter, no Requires,
  and the plist directory scan is not even sorted), so wland must NOT assume
  configd is up when it starts — it opens the SCDynamicStore lazily and tolerates
  NULL, which is the same pattern ipconfigd's sc_publish.c uses.

  StandardErrorPath / StandardOutPath route the log to /var/log/wland.stderr,
  matching the ipconfigd / configd convention; the CI boot test dumps these
  post-boot.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>org.nextbsd.wland</string>
    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/usr/sbin/wland</string>
    </array>
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>
    <key>MachServices</key>
    <dict>
        <key>org.nextbsd.wlan</key>
        <true/>
    </dict>
    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/wland.stderr</string>
    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/var/log/wland.stderr</string>
</dict>
</plist>
